===Etymology 1===
{{root|en|ine-pro|*kret-}}
From {{inh|en|enm|hard}}, from {{inh|en|ang|heard}}, from {{inh|en|gmw-pro|*hard(ī)}}, from {{inh|en|gem-pro|*harduz}}, from {{inh|en|ine-pro||*kort-ús}}, from {{m|ine-pro|*kret-||strong, powerful}}. Cognate with {{cog|de|hart}}, {{cog|sv|hård}}, {{cog|grc|κρατύς}}, {{cog|sa|क्रतु|tr=krátu}}, {{cog|ae|𐬑𐬭𐬀𐬙𐬎}}.

====Adjective====
{{en-adj|er|more}}

# {{lb|en|of material or fluid}} Solid and firm.
#* {{RQ:Rushdie Fury|page=5|passage=Luckily she wasn’t there any more, no one was, when he returned from the Caribbean carnival damp-hatted and soaked through after being caught unprepared by a squall of '''hard''', hot rain.}}
## Resistant to pressure; difficult to break, cut or penetrate.
##: {{ux|en|This bread is so stale and '''hard''', I can barely cut it.}}
## {{lb|en|of drink or drugs}} [[strong|Strong]].
## {{lb|en|of a normally nonalcoholic drink}} Containing [[alcohol]].
##: {{ux|en|'''hard''' cider, '''hard''' lemonade, '''hard''' seltzer, '''hard''' soda}}
##* {{quote-journal|en|date=2023-03-01|author=Rachel Ellison|title=Bad Dates Turn Out to Be Excellent on TikTok|journal=w:The New York Times|url=https://www.nytimes.com/2023/03/01/style/tiktok-dating-ghosting.html|passage=Stunned, she deleted his number and went home. Then she cracked a '''hard''' seltzer, opened her phone’s camera and filmed a TikTok video recounting the evening {{nb...}}.}}
